@node Expressions
@nodedescription Descriptions of Scheme expressions.
@chapter Expressions
Expression types are categorized as @define{primitive} or
@define{derived}.  Primitive expression types include variables and
procedure calls.  Derived expression types are not semantically
primitive, but can instead be defined as macros.  Suitable syntax
definitions of some of the derived expressions are given in section 4.2
(@ref{Derived expression types}).

The procedures @nc{force}, @nc{promise?}, @nc{make-promise}, and
@nc{make-parameter} are also described in this chapter because they are
intimately associated with the @nc{delay}, @nc{delay-force}, and
@nc{parameterize} expression types.

@node Primitive expression types
@section Primitive expression types
@node Variable references
@subsection Variable references
@defblock
@deftypeline syntax @/ @sv{variable}

An expression consisting of a variable (@pxref{Variables syntactic
keywords and regions}) is a variable reference.  The value of the
variable reference is the value stored in the location to which the
variable is bound.  It is an error to reference an unbound variable.

@lisp
(define x 28)
x @ok{28}
@end lisp
@end defblock

@node Literal expressions
@subsection Literal expressions
@defblock
@deftypeline syntax @/ @syntax{quote} @sv{datum}
@deftypeline syntax @/ @syntax{@U{0027}}@sv{datum}
@deftypeline syntax @/ @sv{constant}

@samp{(quote @sv{datum})} evaluates to @samp{@sv{datum}}.
@samp{@sv{Datum}} can be any external representation of a Scheme object
(@pxref{External representations}).  This notation is used to include
literal constants in Scheme code.

@lisp
(quote a)        @ok{a}
(quote #(a b c)) @ok{#(a b c)}
(quote (+ 1 2))  @ok{(+ 1 2)}
@end lisp

@samp{(quote @sv{datum})} can be abbreviated as @samp{'@sv{datum}}.  The
two notations are equivalent in all respects.

@lisp
'a         @ok{a}
'#(a b c)  @ok{#(a b c)}
'()        @ok{()}
'(+ 1 2)   @ok{(+ 1 2)}
'(quote a) @ok{(quote a)}
''a        @ok{(quote a)}
@end lisp

Numerical constants, string constants, character constants, vector
constants, bytevector constants, and boolean constants evaluate to
themselves; they need not be quoted.

@lisp
'145932     @ok{145932}
145932      @ok{145932}
'"abc"      @ok{"abc"}
"abc"       @ok{"abc"}
'#\a        @ok{#\a}
#\a         @ok{#\a}
'#(a 10)    @ok{#(a 10)}
#(a 10)     @ok{#(a 10)}
'#u8(64 65) @ok{#u8(64 65)}
#u8(64 65)  @ok{#u8(64 65)}
'#t         @ok{#t}
#t          @ok{#t}
@end lisp

As noted in section 3.4 (@ref{Storage model}), it is an error to attempt
to alter a constant (i.e.@: the value of a literal expression) using a
mutation procedure like @nc{set-car!} or @nc{string-set!}.
@end defblock

@node Procedure calls
@subsection Procedure calls
@defblock
@deftypeline syntax @/ @t{(@sv{operator} @sv{@parI{operand}} @value{defdots})}
A procedure call is written by enclosing in parentheses an expression
for the procedure to be called followed by expressions for the arguments
to be passed to it.  The operator and operand expressions are evaluated
(in an unspecified order) and the resulting procedure is passed the
resulting arguments.

@lisp
(+ 3 4)           @ok{7}
((if #f + *) 3 4) @ok{12}
@end lisp

The procedures in this document are available as the values of variables
exported by the standard libraries.  For example, the addition and
multiplication procedures in the above examples are the values of the
variables @code{+} and @code{*} in the base library.  New procedures are
created by evaluating @nc{lambda} expressions (@pxref{Procedures}).

Procedure calls can return any number of values (@pxref{Control
features} for a description of @nc{values}).  Most of the procedures
defined in this report return one value or, for procedures such as
@nc{apply}, pass on the values returned by a call to one of their
arguments.  Exceptions are noted in the individual descriptions.

@quotation
@i{Note:} In contrast to other dialects of Lisp, the order of evaluation
is unspecified, and the operator expression and the operand expressions
are always evaluated with the same evaluation rules.

@i{Note:} Although the order of evaluation is otherwise unspecified, the
effect of any concurrent evaluation of the operator and operand
expressions is constrained to be consistent with some sequential order
of evaluation.  The order of evaluation may be chosen differently for
each procedure call.

@i{Note:} In many dialects of Lisp, the empty list, @nc{()}, is a
legitimate expression evaluating to itself.  In Scheme, it is an error.
@end quotation
@end defblock

@node Procedures
@subsection Procedures
@deftypefn syntax @/ lambda @sv{formals} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Formals} is a formal arguments list as described
below, and @angle{body} is a sequence of zero or more definitions
followed by one or more expressions.

@i{Semantics:} A @nc{lambda} expression evaluates to a procedure.  The
environment in effect when the @nc{lambda} expression was evaluated is
remembered as part of the procedure.  When the procedure is later called
with some actual arguments, the environment in which the @nc{lambda}
expression was evaluated will be extended by binding the variables in
the formal argument list to fresh locations, and the corresponding
actual argument values will be stored in those locations.  (A
@define{fresh} location is one that is distinct from every previously
existing location.)  Next, the expressions in the body of the
@nc{lambda} expression (which, if it contains definitions, represents a
@nc{letrec*} form --- @pxref{Binding constructs}) will be evaluated
sequentially in the extended environment.  The results of the last
expression in the body will be returned as the results of the procedure
call.

@lisp
(lambda (x) (+ x x))     @lispnote{a procedure}
((lambda (x) (+ x x)) 4) @ok{8}
@end lisp

@lisp
(define reverse-subtract
  (lambda (x y) (- y x)))
(reverse-subtract 7 10) @ok{3}
@end lisp

@lisp
(define add4
  (let ((x 4))
    (lambda (y) (+ x y))))
(add4 6) @ok{10}
@end lisp

@angle{Formals} have one of the following forms:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@nt{(@sv{@parI{variable}} @value{defdots})}: The procedure takes a fixed
number of arguments; when the procedure is called, the arguments will be
stored in fresh locations that are bound to the corresponding variables.

@item
@nt{@sv{variable}}: The procedure takes any number of arguments; when
the procedure is called, the sequence of actual arguments is converted
into a newly allocated list, and the list is stored in a fresh location
that is bound to @angle{variable}.

@item
@nt{(@sv{@parI{variable}} @value{defdots} @sv{@parn{variable}}
. @sv{@parnplusone{variable}})}: If a space-delimited period precedes
the last variable, then the procedure takes @var{n} or more arguments,
where @var{n} is the number of formal arguments before the period (it is
an error if there is not at least one).  The value stored in the binding
of the last variable will be a newly allocated list of the actual
arguments left over after all the other actual arguments have been
matched up against the other formal arguments.
@end itemize

It is an error for a @angle{variable} to appear more than once in
@angle{formals}.

@lisp
((lambda x x) 3 4 5 6) @ok{(3 4 5 6)}
((lambda (x y . z) z)
 3 4 5 6)              @ok{(5 6)}
@end lisp

Each procedure created as the result of evaluating a @nc{lambda}
expression is (conceptually) tagged with a storage location, in order to
make @nc{eqv?} and @nc{eq?} work on procedures (@pxref{Equivalence
predicates}).
@end deftypefn

@node Primitive Conditionals
@subsection Conditionals
@deftypefn syntax @/ if @sv{test} @sv{consequent} @sv{alternate}
@deftypefnx syntax @/ if @sv{test} @sv{consequent}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Test}, @angle{consequent}, and @angle{alternate} are
expressions.

@i{Semantics:} An @nc{if} expression is evaluated as follows: first,
@angle{test} is evaluated.  If it yields a true value
(@pxref{Booleans}), then @angle{consequent} is evaluated and its values
are returned.  Otherwise @angle{alternate} is evaluated and its values
are returned.  If @angle{test} yields a false value and no
@angle{alternate} is specified, then the result of the expression is
unspecified.

@lisp
(if (> 3 2) 'yes 'no) @ok{yes}
@end lisp

@lisp
(if (> 2 3) 'yes 'no) @ok{no}
@end lisp

@lisp
(if (> 3 2)
    (- 3 2)
    (+ 3 2))          @ok{1}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@node Assignments
@subsection Assignments
@deftypefn syntax @/ set! @sv{variable} @sv{expression}

@i{Semantics:} @angle{Expression} is evaluated, and the resulting value
is stored in the location to which @angle{variable} is bound.  It is an
error if @angle{variable} is not bound either in some region enclosing
the @nc{set!}@: expression or else globally.  The result of the
@nc{set!}@: expression is unspecified.

@lisp
(define x 2)
(+ x 1)    @ok{3}
(set! x 4) @unspecified{unspecified}
(+ x 1)    @ok{5}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@node Inclusion
@subsection Inclusion
@deftypefn syntax @/ include @sv{@parI{string}} @sv{@parII{string}} @value{defdots}
@deftypefnx syntax @/ include-ci @sv{@parI{string}} @sv{@parII{string}} @value{defdots}

@i{Semantics:} Both @nc{include} and @nc{include-ci} take one or more
filenames expressed as string literals, apply an implementation-specific
algorithm to find corresponding files, read the contents of the files in
the specified order as if by repeated applications of @nc{read}, and
effectively replace the @nc{include} or @nc{include-ci} expression with
a @nc{begin} expression containing what was read from the files.  The
difference between the two is that @nc{include-ci} reads each file as if
it began with the @nc{#!fold-case} directive, while @nc{include} does
not.

@quotation
@i{Note:} Implementations are encouraged to search for files in the
directory which contains the including file, and to provide a way for
users to specify other directories to search.
@end quotation
@end deftypefn

@node Derived expression types
@section Derived expression types
The constructs in this section are hygienic, as discussed in section 4.3
(@ref{Macros}).  For reference purposes, section 7.2 (@ref{Derived
expression types}) gives syntax definitions that will convert most of
the constructs described in this section into the primitive constructs
described in the previous section.

@node Derived Conditionals
@subsection Conditionals
@deftypefn syntax @/ cond @sv{@parI{clause}} @sv{@parII{clause}} @value{defdots}
@deftypefnx {auxiliary syntax} @/ else
@deftypefnx {auxiliary syntax} @/ =>

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Clause}s take one of two forms, either

@example grammar
(@sv{test} @sv{@parI{expression}} @value{defdots})
@end example

where @angle{test} is any expression, or

@example grammar
(@sv{test} @aux{=>} @sv{expression})
@end example

The last @angle{clause} can be an @quotedbl{else clause}, which has the form

@example grammar
(else @sv{@parI{expression}} @sv{@parII{expression}} @value{defdots})@r{.}
@end example

@i{Semantics:} A @nc{cond} expression is evaluated by evaluating the
@angle{test} expressions of successive @angle{clause}s in order until
one of them evaluates to a true value (@pxref{Booleans}).  When a
@angle{test} evaluates to a true value, the remaining
@angle{expression}s in its @angle{clause} are evaluated in order, and
the results of the last @angle{expression} in the @angle{clause} are
returned as the results of the entire @nc{cond} expression.

If the selected @angle{clause} contains only the @angle{test} and no
@angle{expression}s, then the value of the @angle{test} is returned as
the result.  If the selected @angle{clause} uses the @nc{=>} alternate
form, then the @angle{expression} is evaluated.  It is an error if its
value is not a procedure that accepts one argument.  This procedure is
then called on the value of the @angle{test} and the values returned by
this procedure are returned by the @nc{cond} expression.

If all @angle{test}s evaluate to @nc{#f}, and there is no else clause,
then the result of the conditional expression is unspecified; if there
is an else clause, then its @angle{expression}s are evaluated in order,
and the values of the last one are returned.

@lisp
(cond ((> 3 2) 'greater)
      ((< 3 2) 'less)) @ok{greater}
@end lisp

@lisp
(cond ((> 3 3) 'greater)
      ((< 3 3) 'less)
      (else 'equal) @ok{equal}
@end lisp

@lisp
(cond ((assv 'b '((a 1) (b 2))) => cadr)
      (else #f) @ok{2}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ case @sv{key} @sv{@parI{clause}} @sv{@parII{clause}} @value{defdots}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Key} can be any expression.  Each @angle{clause} has
the form

@example grammar
((@sv{@parI{datum}} @value{defdots}) @sv{@parI{expression}} @sv{@parII{expression} @value{defdots}})@r{,}
@end example

where each @angle{datum} is an external representation of some object.
It is an error if any of the @angle{datum}s are the same anywhere in the
expression.  Alternatively, a @angle{clause} can be of the form

@example grammar
((@sv{@parI{datum}} @value{defdots}) @aux{=>} @sv{expression})
@end example

The last @angle{clause} can be an @quotedbl{else clause}, which has one
of the forms

@example grammar
(else @sv{@parI{expression}} @sv{@parII{expression}} @value{defdots})
@end example

or

@example grammar
(else @aux{=>} @sv{expression})@r{.}
@end example

@i{Semantics:} A @nc{case} expression is evaluated as follows.
@angle{Key} is evaluated and its result is compared against each
@angle{datum}.  If the result of evaluating @angle{key} is the same (in
the sense of @nc{eqv?}; @pxref{Equivalence predicates}) to a
@angle{datum}, then the expressions in the corresponding @angle{clause}
are evaluated in order and the results of the last expression in the
@angle{clause} are returned as the results of the @nc{case} expression.

If the result of evaluating @angle{key} is different from every
@angle{datum}, then if there is an else clause, its expressions are
evaluated and the results of the last are the results of the @nc{case}
expression; otherwise the result of the @nc{case} expression is
unspecified.

If the selected @angle{clause} or else clause uses the @nc{=>} alternate
form, then the @angle{expression} is evaluated.  It is an error if its
value is not a procedure accepting one argument.  This procedure is then
called on the value of the @angle{key} and the values returned by this
procedure are returned by the @nc{case} expression.

@lisp
(case (* 2 3)
  ((2 3 5 7) 'prime)
  ((1 4 6 8 9) 'composite)) @ok{composite}
@end lisp

@lisp
(case (car '(c d))
  ((a) 'a)
  ((b) 'b)) @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp

@lisp
(case (car '(c d))
  ((a e i o u) 'vowel)
  ((w y) 'semivowel)
  (else => (lambda (x) x))) @ok{c}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ and @sv{@parI{test}} @value{defdots}

@i{Semantics:} The @angle{test} expressions are evaluated from left to
right, and if any expression evaluates to @nc{#f} (@pxref{Booleans}),
then @nc{#f} is returned.  Any remaining expressions are not evaluated.
If all the expressions evaluate to true values, the values of the last
expression are returned.  If there are no expressions, then @nc{#t} is
returned.

@lisp
(and (= 2 2) (> 2 1)) @ok{#t}
(and (= 2 2) (< 2 1)) @ok{#f}
(and 1 2 'c '(f g))   @ok{(f g)}
(and)                 @ok{#t}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ or @sv{@parI{test}} @value{defdots}

@i{Semantics:} The @angle{test} expressions are evaluated from left to
right, and the value of the first expression that evaluates to a true
value (@pxref{Booleans}) is returned.  Any remaining expressions are not
evaluated.  If all expressions evaluate to @nc{#f} or if there are no
expressions, then @nc{#f} is returned.

@lisp
(or (= 2 2) (> 2 1))   @ok{#t}
(or (= 2 2) (< 2 1))   @ok{#t}
(or #f #f #f)          @ok{#f}
(or (memq 'b '(a b c))
    (/ 3 0))           @ok{(b c)}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ when @
@sv{test} @sv{@parI{expression}} @sv{@parII{expression}} @value{defdots}

@i{Syntax:} The @angle{test} is an expression.

@i{Semantics:} The test is evaluated, and if it evaluates to a true
value, the expressions are evaluated in order.  The result of the
@nc{when} expression is unspecified.

@ifinfo
@lisp
(when (= 1 1.0)
  (display "1")
  (display "2")) @output{12}
                 @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp
@end ifinfo
@ifnotinfo
@lisp
(when (= 1 1.0)
  (display "1")
  (display "2")) @unspecified{unspecified}
           @andprints{12}
@end lisp
@end ifnotinfo
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ unless @
@sv{test} @sv{@parI{expression}} @sv{@parII{expression}} @value{defdots}

@i{Syntax:} The @angle{test} is an expression.

@i{Semantics:} The test is evaluated, and if it evaluates to @nc{#f},
the expressions are evaluated in order.  The result of the @nc{unless}
expression is unspecified.

@ifinfo
@lisp
(unless (= 1 1.0)
  (display "1")
  (display "2")) @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp
@end ifinfo
@ifnotinfo
@lisp
(unless (= 1 1.0)
  (display "1")
  (display "2")) @unspecified{unspecified}
           @unspecifiedtext{and prints nothing}
@end lisp
@end ifnotinfo
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ cond-expand @
@sv{@parI{ce-clause}} @sv{@parII{ce-clause}} @value{defdots}

@i{Syntax:} The @nc{cond-expand} expression type provides a way to
statically expand different expressions depending on the implementation.
A @angle{ce-clause} takes the following form:

@example grammar
(@sv{feature requirement} @sv{expression} @value{defdots})
@end example

The last clause can be an @quotedbl{else clause}, which has the form
@example grammar
(else @sv{expression} @value{defdots})
@end example

A @angle{feature requirement} takes one of the following forms:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@t{@sv{feature identifier}}

@item
@nt{@compound{@U{0028}}@aux{library} @sv{library name}@compound{@U{0029}}}

@item
@nt{@compound{@U{0028}}@aux{and} @sv{feature requirement} @value{defdots}@compound{@U{0029}}}

@item
@nt{@compound{@U{0028}}@aux{or} @sv{feature requirement} @value{defdots}@compound{@U{0029}}}

@item
@nt{@compound{@U{0028}}@aux{not} @sv{feature requirement}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@end itemize

@i{Semantics:} Each implementation maintains a list of feature
identifiers which are present, as well as a list of libraries which can
be imported.  The value of a @angle{feature requirement} is determined
by replacing each @angle{feature identifier} and @nt{(library
@sv{library name})} on the implementation's lists with @nc{#t}, and all
other feature identifiers and library names with @nc{#f}, then
evaluating the resulting expression as a Scheme boolean expression under
the normal interpretation of @nc{and}, @nc{or}, and @nc{not}.

A @nc{cond-expand} is then expanded by evaluating the @angle{feature
requirement}s of successive @angle{ce-clause}s in order until one of
them returns @nc{#t}.  When a true clause is found, the corresponding
@angle{expression}s are expanded to a @nc{begin}, and the remaining
clauses are ignored.  If none of the @angle{feature requirement}s
evaluate to @nc{#t}, then if there is an else clause, its
@angle{expression}s are included.  Otherwise, the behavior of the
@nc{cond-expand} is unspecified.  Unlike @nc{cond}, @nc{cond-expand}
does not depend on the value of any variables.

The exact features provided are implementation-defined, but for
portability a core set of features is given in Appendix B (@ref{Standard
Feature Identifiers}).
@end deftypefn

@node Binding constructs
@subsection Binding constructs
The binding constructs @nc{let}, @nc{let*}, @nc{letrec}, @nc{letrec*},
@nc{let-values}, and @nc{let*-values} give Scheme a block structure,
like Algol 60.  The syntax of the first four constructs is identical,
but they differ in the regions they establish for their variable
bindings.  In a @nc{let} expression, the initial values are computed
before any of the variables become bound; in a @nc{let*} expression, the
bindings and evaluations are performed sequentially; while in
@nc{letrec} and @nc{letrec*} expressions, all the bindings are in effect
while their initial values are being computed, thus allowing mutually
recursive definitions.  The @nc{let-values} and @nc{let*-values}
constructs are analogous to @nc{let} and @nc{let*} respectively, but are
designed to handle multiple-valued expressions, binding different
identifiers to the returned values.

@deftypefn syntax @/ let @sv{bindings} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Bindings} has the form

@example grammar
((@sv{@parI{variable}} @sv{@parI{init}}) @value{defdots})@r{,}
@end example

where each @angle{init} is an expression, and @angle{body} is a sequence
of zero or more definitions followed by a sequence of one or more
expressions as described in section 4.1.4 (@ref{Procedures}).  It is an
error for a @angle{variable} to appear more than once in the list of
variables being bound.

@i{Semantics:} The @angle{init}s are evaluated in the current
environment (in some unspecified order), the @angle{variable}s are bound
to fresh locations holding the results, the @angle{body} is evaluated in
the extended environment, and the values of the last expression of
@angle{body} are returned.  Each binding of a @angle{variable} has
@angle{body} as its region.

@lisp
(let ((x 2) (y 3))
  (* x y)) @ok{6}
@end lisp

@lisp
(let ((x 2) (y 3))
  (let ((x 7)
        (z (+ x y)))
    (* z x))) @ok{35}
@end lisp

@xref{Iteration} for a description of @quotedbl{named @nt{let}}.
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ let* @sv{bindings} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Bindings} has the form

@example grammar
((@sv{@parI{variable}} @sv{@parI{init}}) @value{defdots})@r{,}
@end example

and @angle{body} is a sequence of zero or more definitions followed by
one or more expressions as described in section 4.1.4
(@ref{Procedures}).

@i{Semantics:} The @nc{let*} binding construct is similar to @nc{let},
but the bindings are performed sequentially from left to right, and the
region of a binding indicated by @nt{(@sv{variable} @sv{init})} is that
part of the @nc{let*} expression to the right of the binding.  Thus the
second binding is done in an environment in which the first binding is
visible, and so on.  The @angle{variable}s need not be distinct.
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ letrec @sv{bindings} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Bindings} has the form

@example grammar
((@sv{@parI{variable}} @sv{@parI{init}}) @value{defdots})@r{,}
@end example

and @angle{body} is a sequence of zero or more definitions followed by
one or more expressions as described in section 4.1.4
(@ref{Procedures}).  It is an error for a @angle{variable} to appear
more than once in the list of variables being bound.

@i{Semantics:} The @angle{variable}s are bound to fresh locations
holding unspecified values, the @angle{init}s are evaluated in the
resulting environment (in some unspecified order), each @angle{variable}
is assigned to the result of the corresponding @angle{init}, the
@angle{body} is evaluated in the resulting environment, and the values
of the last expression in @angle{body} are returned.  Each binding of a
@angle{variable} has the entire @nc{letrec} expression as its region,
making it possible to define mutually recursive procedures.

@lisp
(letrec ((even?
          (lambda (n)
            (if (zero? n)
                #t
                (odd? (- n 1)))))
         (odd?
          (lambda (n)
            (if (zero? n)
                #f
                (even? (- n 1))))))
  (even? 88)) @ok{#t}
@end lisp

One restriction on @nc{letrec} is very important: if it is not possible
to evaluate each @angle{init} without assigning or referring to the
value of any @angle{variable}, it is an error.  The restriction is
necessary because @nc{letrec} is defined in terms of a procedure call
where a @nc{lambda} expression binds the @angle{variable}s to the values
of the @angle{init}s.  In the most common uses of @nc{letrec}, all the
@angle{init}s are @nc{lambda} expressions and the restriction is
satisfied automatically.
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ letrec* @sv{bindings} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Bindings} has the form

@example grammar
((@sv{@parI{variable}} @sv{@parI{init}}) @value{defdots})@r{,}
@end example

and @angle{body} is a sequence of zero or more definitions followed by
one or more expressions as described in section 4.1.4
(@ref{Procedures}).  It is an error for a @angle{variable} to appear
more than once in the list of variables being bound.

@i{Semantics:} The @angle{variable}s are bound to fresh locations, each
@angle{variable} is assigned in left-to-right order to the result of
evaluating the corresponding @angle{init} (interleaving evaluations and
assignments), the @angle{body} is evaluated in the resulting
environment, and the values of the last expression in @angle{body} are
returned.  Despite the left-to-right evaluation and assignment order,
each binding of a @angle{variable} has the entire @nc{letrec*}
expression as its region, making it possible to define mutually
recursive procedures.

If it is not possible to evaluate each @angle{init} without assigning or
referring to the value of the corresponding @angle{variable} or the
@angle{variable} of any of the bindings that follow it in
@angle{bindings}, it is an error.  Another restriction is that it is an
error to invoke the continuation of an @angle{init} more than once.

@lisp
;; Returns the arithmetic, geometric, and
;; harmonic means of a nested list of numbers
(define (means ton)
  (letrec*
     ((mean
        (lambda (f g)
          (f (/ (sum g ton) n))))
      (sum
        (lambda (g ton)
          (if (null? ton)
            (+)
            (if (number? ton)
                (g ton)
                (+ (sum g (car ton))
                   (sum g (cdr ton)))))))
      (n (sum (lambda (x) 1) ton)))
    (values (mean values values)
            (mean exp log)
            (mean / /))))
@end lisp

Evaluating @nc{(means '(3 (1 4)))} returns three
values:@inlinefmt{html,@*} @nc{8/3}, @nc{2.28942848510666}
(approximately), and @nc{36/19}.
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ let-values @sv{mv binding spec} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Mv binding spec} has the form

@example grammar
((@sv{@parI{formals}} @sv{@parI{init}}) @value{defdots})@r{,}
@end example

where each @angle{init} is an expression, and @angle{body} is zero or
more definitions followed by a sequence of one or more expressions as
described in section 4.1.4 (@ref{Procedures}).  It is an error for a
variable to appear more than once in the set of @angle{formals}.

Semantics: The @angle{init}s are evaluated in the current environment
(in some unspecified order) as if by invoking @nc{call-with-values}, and
the variables occurring in the @angle{formals} are bound to fresh
locations holding the values returned by the @angle{init}s, where the
@angle{formals} are matched to the return values in the same way that
the @angle{formals} in a @nc{lambda} expression are matched to the
arguments in a procedure call.  Then, the @angle{body} is evaluated in
the extended environment, and the values of the last expression of
@angle{body} are returned.  Each binding of a @angle{variable} has
@angle{body} as its region.

It is an error if the @angle{formals} do not match the number of values
returned by the corresponding @angle{init}.

@lisp
(let-values (((root rem) (exact-integer-sqrt 32)))
  (* root rem)) @ok{35}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ let*-values @sv{mv binding spec} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Mv binding spec} has the form

@example grammar
((@sv{formals} @sv{init}) @value{defdots})@r{,}
@end example

and @angle{body} is a sequence of zero or more definitions followed by
one or more expressions as described in section 4.1.4
(@ref{Procedures}).  In each @angle{formals}, it is an error if any
variable appears more than once.

@i{Semantics:} The @nc{let*-values} construct is similar to
@nc{let-values}, but the @angle{init}s are evaluated and bindings
created sequentially from left to right, with the region of the bindings
of each @angle{formals} including the @angle{init}s to its right as well
as @angle{body}.  Thus the second @angle{init} is evaluated in an
environment in which the first set of bindings is visible and
initialized, and so on.

@lisp
(let ((a 'a) (b 'b) (x 'x) (y 'y))
  (let*-values (((a b) (values x y))
                ((x y) (values a b)))
    (list a b x y))) @ok{(x y x y)}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@node Sequencing
@subsection Sequencing
Both of Scheme's sequencing constructs are named @nc{begin}, but the two
have slightly different forms and uses:

@deftypefn syntax @/ begin @sv{expression or definition} @value{defdots}

This form of @nc{begin} can appear as part of a @angle{body}, or at the
outermost level of a @angle{program}, or at the
@acronym{REPL,Read-Eval-Print Loop}, or directly nested in a @nc{begin}
that is itself of this form.  It causes the contained expressions and
definitions to be evaluated exactly as if the enclosing @nc{begin}
construct were not present.

@quotation
@i{Rationale:} This form is commonly used in the output of macros
(@pxref{Macros}) which need to generate multiple definitions and splice
them into the context in which they are expanded.
@end quotation
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ begin @
@sv{@parI{expression}} @sv{@parII{expression}} @value{defdots}

This form of @nc{begin} can be used as an ordinary expression.  The
@angle{expression}s are evaluated sequentially from left to right, and
the values of the last @angle{expression} are returned.  This expression
type is used to sequence side effects such as assignments or input and
output.

@lisp
(define x 0)
(and (= x 0)
     (begin (set! x 5)
            (+ x 1))) @ok{6}
@end lisp

@ifinfo
@lisp
(begin (display "4 plus 1 equals ")
       (display (+ 4 1))) @output{4 plus 1 equals 5}
                          @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp
@end ifinfo
@ifnotinfo
@lisp
(begin (display "4 plus 1 equals ")
       (display (+ 4 1))) @unspecified{unspecified}
           @andprints{4 plus 1 equals 5}
@end lisp
@end ifnotinfo

Note that there is a third form of @nc{begin} used as a library
declaration: @pxref{Library syntax}.
@end deftypefn

@node Iteration
@subsection Iteration
@deftypefn syntax @/ do @
@t{((@sv{@parI{variable}} @sv{@parI{init}} @sv{@parI{step}})@*@
@value{syntaxindent}@value{cdspace}@
@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@
@value{defdots})@*@
@value{syntaxindent}@value{cdspace}@
@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@
(@sv{test} @sv{expression} @value{defdots})@*@
@value{syntaxindent}@value{cdspace}@
@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@
@sv{command} @value{defdots}}

@i{Syntax:} All of @angle{init}, @angle{step}, @angle{test}, and
@angle{command} are expressions.

@i{Semantics:} A @nc{do} expression is an iteration construct.  It
specifies a set of variables to be bound, how they are to be initialized
at the start, and how they are to be updated on each iteration.  When a
termination condition is met, the loop exits after evaluating the
@angle{expression}s.

A @nc{do} expression is evaluated as follows: The @angle{init}
expressions are evaluated (in some unspecified order), the
@angle{variable}s are bound to fresh locations, the results of the
@angle{init} expressions are stored in the bindings of the
@angle{variable}s, and then the iteration phase begins.

Each iteration begins by evaluating @angle{test}; if the result is false
(@pxref{Booleans}), then the @angle{command} expressions are evaluated
in order for effect, the @angle{step} expressions are evaluated in some
unspecified order, the @angle{variable}s are bound to fresh locations,
the results of the @angle{step}s are stored in the bindings of the
@angle{variable}s, and the next iteration begins.

If @angle{test} evaluates to a true value, then the @angle{expression}s
are evaluated from left to right and the values of the last
@angle{expression} are returned.  If no @angle{expression}s are present,
then the value of the @nc{do} expression is unspecified.

The region of the binding of a @angle{variable} consists of the entire
@nc{do} expression except for the @angle{init}s.  It is an error for a
@angle{variable} to appear more than once in the list of @nc{do}
variables.

A @angle{step} can be omitted, in which case the effect is the same as
if @nt{(@sv{variable} @sv{init} @sv{variable})} had been written instead
of @nt{(@sv{variable} @sv{init})}.

@lisp
(do ((vec (make-vector 5))
     (i 0 (+ i 1)))
    ((= i 5) vec)
  (vector-set! vec i i)) @ok{#(0 1 2 3 4)}
@end lisp

@lisp
(let ((x '(1 3 5 7 9)))
  (do ((x x (cdr x))
       (sum 0 (+ sum (car x))))
      ((null? x) sum))) @ok{25}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ let @sv{variable} @sv{bindings} @sv{body}

@i{Semantics:} @quotedbl{Named @nt{let}} is a variant on the syntax of
@nc{let} which provides a more general looping construct than @nc{do}
and can also be used to express recursion.  It has the same syntax and
semantics as ordinary @nc{let} except that @angle{variable} is bound
within @angle{body} to a procedure whose formal arguments are the bound
variables and whose body is @angle{body}.  Thus the execution of
@angle{body} can be repeated by invoking the procedure named by
@angle{variable}.

@lisp
(let loop ((numbers '(3 -2 1 6 -5))
           (nonneg '())
           (neg '()))
  (cond ((null? numbers) (list nonneg neg))
        ((>= (car numbers) 0)
         (loop (cdr numbers)
               (cons (car numbers) nonneg)
               neg))
        ((< (car numbers) 0)
         (loop (cdr numbers)
               nonneg
               (cons (car numbers) neg)))))
          @ok{((6 1 3) (-5 -2))}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@node Delayed evaluation
@subsection Delayed evaluation
@deftypefn {lazy library syntax} @/ delay @sv{expression}

@i{Semantics:} The @nc{delay} construct is used together with the
procedure @nc{force} to implement lazy evaluation or call by need.
@nt{(delay @sv{expression})} returns an object called a @define{promise}
which at some point in the future can be asked (by the @nc{force}
procedure) to evaluate @angle{expression}, and deliver the resulting
value.  The effect of @angle{expression} returning multiple values is
unspecified.
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn {lazy library syntax} @/ delay-force @sv{expression}

@i{Semantics:} The expression @nt{(delay-force @r{@var{expression}})} is
conceptually similar to @nt{(delay (force @r{@var{expression}}))}, with
the difference that forcing the result of @nc{delay-force} will in
effect result in a tail call to @nt{(force @r{@var{expression}})}, while
forcing the result of @nt{(delay (force @r{@var{expression}}))} might
not.  Thus iterative lazy algorithms that might result in a long series
of chains of @nc{delay} and @nc{force} can be rewritten using
@nc{delay-force} to prevent consuming unbounded space during evaluation.
@end deftypefn

@deffn {lazy library procedure} force promise

The @nc{force} procedure forces the value of a @i{promise} created by
@nc{delay}, @nc{delay-force}, or @nc{make-promise}.  If no value has
been computed for the promise, then a value is computed and returned.
The value of the promise must be cached (or @quotedbl{memoized}) so that
if it is forced a second time, the previously computed value is
returned.  Consequently, a delayed expression is evaluated using the
parameter values and exception handler of the call to @nc{force} which
first requested its value.  If @var{promise} is not a promise, it may be
returned unchanged.

@lisp
(force (delay (+ 1 2))) @ok{3}
@end lisp

@lisp
(let ((p (delay (+ 1 2))))
  (list (force p) (force p))) @ok{(3 3)}
@end lisp

@lisp
(define integers
  (letrec ((next
            (lambda (n)
              (delay (cons n (next (+ n 1)))))))
    (next 0)))
(define head
  (lambda (stream) (car (force stream))))
(define tail
  (lambda (stream) (cdr (force stream))))

(head (tail (tail integers))) @ok{2}
@end lisp

The following example is a mechanical transformation of a lazy
stream-filtering algorithm into Scheme.  Each call to a constructor is
wrapped in @nc{delay}, and each argument passed to a deconstructor is
wrapped in @nc{force}.  The use of @nt{(delay-force @value{defdots})}@:
instead of @nt{(delay (force @value{defdots}))} around the body of the
procedure ensures that an ever-growing sequence of pending promises does
not exhaust available storage, because @nc{force} will in effect force
such sequences iteratively.

@lisp
(define (stream-filter p? s)
  (delay-force
   (if (null? (force s))
       (delay '())
       (let ((h (car (force s)))
             (t (cdr (force s))))
         (if (p? h)
             (delay (cons h (stream-filter p? t)))
             (stream-filter p? t))))))

(head (tail (tail (stream-filter odd? integers)))) @ok{5}
@end lisp

The following examples are not intended to illustrate good programming
style, as @nc{delay}, @nc{force}, and @nc{delay-force} are mainly
intended for programs written in the functional style.  However, they do
illustrate the property that only one value is computed for a promise,
no matter how many times it is forced.

@lisp
(define count 0)
(define p
  (delay (begin (set! count (+ count 1))
                (if (> count x)
                    count
                    (force p)))))
(define x 5)
p                  @lispnote{a promise}
(force p)          @ok{6}
p                  @lispnote{a promise, still}
(begin (set! x 10)
       (force p))  @ok{6}
@end lisp

Various extensions to this semantics of @nc{delay}, @nc{force}, and
@nc{delay-force} are supported in some implementations:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Calling @nc{force} on an object that is not a promise may simply return
the object.

@item
It may be the case that there is no means by which a promise can be
operationally distinguished from its forced value.  That is, expressions
like the following may evaluate to either @nc{#t} or to @nc{#f},
depending on the implementation:

@lisp
(eqv? (delay 1) 1)         @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp

@lisp
(pair? (delay (cons 1 2))) @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp

@item
Implementations may implement @quotedbl{implicit forcing}, where the
value of a promise is forced by procedures that operate only on
arguments of a certain type, like @nc{cdr} and @code{*}.  However,
procedures that operate uniformly on their arguments, like @nc{list},
must not force them.

@lisp
(+ (delay (* 3 7)) 13)       @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp

@lisp
(car
  (list (delay (* 3 7)) 13)) @lispnote{a promise}
@end lisp
@end itemize
@end deffn

@deffn {lazy library procedure} promise? obj

The @nc{promise?} procedure returns @nc{#t} if its argument is a
promise, and @nc{#f} otherwise.  Note that promises are not necessarily
disjoint from other Scheme types such as procedures.
@end deffn

@deffn {lazy library procedure} make-promise obj

The @nc{make-promise} procedure returns a promise which, when forced,
will return @var{obj}.  It is similar to @nc{delay}, but does not delay
its argument: it is a procedure rather than syntax.  If @var{obj} is
already a promise, it is returned.
@end deffn

@node Dynamic bindings
@subsection Dynamic bindings
The @define{dynamic extent} of a procedure call is the time between when
it is initiated and when it returns.  In Scheme,
@nc{call-with-current-continuation} (@pxref{Control features}) allows
reentering a dynamic extent after its procedure call has returned.
Thus, the dynamic extent of a call might not be a single, continuous
time period.

This section introduces @define{parameter objects}, which can be bound
to new values for the duration of a dynamic extent.  The set of all
parameter bindings at a given time is called the @define{dynamic
environment}.

@deffn procedure make-parameter init
@deffnx procedure make-parameter init converter

Returns a newly allocated parameter object, which is a procedure that
accepts zero arguments and returns the value associated with the
parameter object.  Initially, this value is the value of
@nt{(@r{@var{converter}} @r{@var{init}})}, or @var{init} if the
conversion procedure @var{converter} is not specified.  The associated
value can be temporarily changed using @nc{parameterize}, which is
described below.

The effect of passing arguments to a parameter object is
implementation-dependent.
@end deffn

@deftypefn syntax @/ parameterize @
@t{((@sv{@parI{param}} @sv{@parI{value}}) @value{defdots})@*@
@value{syntaxindent}@value{cdspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@sv{body}}

@i{Syntax:} Both @angle{@parI{param}} and @angle{@parI{value}} are
expressions.

It is an error if the value of any @angle{param} expression is not a
parameter object.

@i{Semantics:} A @nc{parameterize} expression is used to change the
values returned by specified parameter objects during the evaluation of
the body.

The @angle{param} and @angle{value} expressions are evaluated in an
unspecified order.  The @angle{body} is evaluated in a dynamic
environment in which calls to the parameters return the results of
passing the corresponding values to the conversion procedure specified
when the parameters were created.  Then the previous values of the
parameters are restored without passing them to the conversion
procedure.  The results of the last expression in the @angle{body} are
returned as the results of the entire @nc{parameterize} expression.

@quotation
@i{Note:} If the conversion procedure is not idempotent, the results of
@code{(@nobr{parameterize} ((x (x))) ...)}, which appears to bind the
parameter @code{x} to its current value, might not be what the user
expects.
@end quotation

If an implementation supports multiple threads of execution, then
@nc{parameterize} must not change the associated values of any
parameters in any thread other than the current thread and threads
created inside @angle{body}.

Parameter objects can be used to specify configurable settings for a
computation without the need to pass the value to every procedure in the
call chain explicitly.

@lisp
(define radix
  (make-parameter
   10
   (lambda (x)
     (if (and (exact-integer? x) (<= 2 x 16))
         x
         (error "invalid radix")))))

(define (f n) (number->string n (radix)))

(f 12)                        @ok{"12"}
(parameterize ((radix 2))
  (f 12))                     @ok{"1100"}
(f 12)                        @ok{"12"}

(radix 16)                    @unspecified{unspecified}

(parameterize ((radix 0))
  (f 12))                     @lispnote{error}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@node Exception handling
@subsection Exception handling
@deftypefn syntax @/ guard @t{(@sv{variable}@*@
@value{syntaxindent}@value{cdspace}@
@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@
@ @ @ @
@sv{@parI{cond clause}} @
@sv{@parII{cond clause}} @value{defdots})@*@
@value{syntaxindent}@value{cdspace}@
@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@sv{body}}

@i{Syntax:} Each @angle{cond clause} is as in the specification of
@nc{cond}.

@i{Semantics:} The @angle{body} is evaluated with an exception handler
that binds the raised object (@pxref{Exceptions} for a description of
@nc{raise}) to @angle{variable} and, within the scope of that binding,
evaluates the clauses as if they were the clauses of a @nc{cond}
expression.  That implicit @nc{cond} expression is evaluated with the
continuation and dynamic environment of the @nc{guard} expression.  If
every @angle{cond clause}'s @angle{test} evaluates to @nc{#f} and there
is no else clause, then @nc{raise-continuable} is invoked on the raised
object within the dynamic environment of the original call to @nc{raise}
or @nc{raise-continuable}, except that the current exception handler is
that of the @nc{guard} expression.

@xref{Exceptions} for a more complete discussion of exceptions.

@lisp
(guard (condition
         ((assq 'a condition) => cdr)
         ((assq 'b condition)))
  (raise (list (cons 'a 42))))
                             @ok{42}
@end lisp

@lisp
(guard (condition
         ((assq 'a condition) => cdr)
         ((assq 'b condition)))
  (raise (list (cons 'b 23))))
                             @ok{(b . 23)}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@node Quasiquotation
@subsection Quasiquotation
@defblock
@deftypeline syntax @/ @syntax{quasiquote} @sv{qq template}
@deftypeline syntax @/ @syntax{@U{0060}}@sv{datum}
@deftypeline {auxiliary syntax} @/ @aux{unquote}
@deftypeline {auxiliary syntax} @/ @aux{@comma{}}
@deftypeline {auxiliary syntax} @/ @aux{unquote-splicing}
@deftypeline {auxiliary syntax} @/ @aux{@comma{}@atchar{}}

@quotedbl{Quasiquote} expressions are useful for constructing a list or
vector structure when some but not all of the desired structure is known
in advance.  If no commas appear within the @nobr{@angle{qq template}},
the result of evaluating @ns{@U{0060}@sv{qq template}} is equivalent to
the result of evaluating @ns{@U{0027}@sv{qq template}}.  If a comma
appears within the @nobr{@angle{qq template}}, however, the expression
following the comma is evaluated (@quotedbl{unquoted}) and its result is
inserted into the structure instead of the comma and the expression.  If
a comma appears followed without intervening whitespace by a commercial
@nobr{at-sign} (@samp{@atchar{}}), then it is an error if the following
expression does not evaluate to a list; the opening and closing
parentheses of the list are then @quotedbl{stripped away} and the
elements of the list are inserted in place of the comma @nobr{at-sign}
expression sequence.  A comma @nobr{at-sign} normally appears only
within a list or vector @nobr{@angle{qq template}}.

@quotation
@i{Note:} In order to unquote an identifier beginning with
@samp{@atchar{}}, it is necessary to use either an explicit
@code{unquote} or to put whitespace after the comma, to avoid colliding
with the comma @nobr{at-sign} sequence.
@end quotation

@lisp
`(list ,(+ 1 2) 4) @ok{(list 3 4)}
@end lisp

@lisp
(let ((name 'a)) `(list ,name ',name))
             @ok{(list a (quote a))}
@end lisp

@lisp
`(a ,(+ 1 2) ,@atchar{}(map abs '(4 -5 6)) b)
             @ok{(a 3 4 5 6 b)}
@end lisp

@lisp
`(( foo ,(- 10 3)) ,@atchar{}(cdr '(c)) . ,(car '(cons)))
             @ok{((foo 7) . cons)}
@end lisp

@lisp
`#(10 5 ,(sqrt 4) ,@atchar{}(map sqrt '(16 9)) 8)
             @ok{#(10 5 2 4 3 8)}
@end lisp

@lisp
(let ((foo '(foo bar)) (@atchar{}baz 'baz))
  `(list ,@atchar{}foo , @atchar{}baz))
             @ok{(list foo bar baz)}
@end lisp

Quasiquote expressions can be nested.  Substitutions are made only for
unquoted components appearing at the same nesting level as the outermost
quasiquote.  The nesting level increases by one inside each successive
quasiquotation, and decreases by one inside each unquotation.

@lisp
`(a `(b ,(+ 1 2) ,(foo ,(+ 1 3) d) e) f)
             @ok{(a `(b ,(+ 1 2) ,(foo 4 d) e) f)}
@end lisp

@lisp
(let ((name1 'x)
      (name2 'y))
  `(a `(b ,,name1 ,',name2 d) e))
             @ok{(a `(b ,x ,'y d) e)}
@end lisp

A quasiquote expression may return either newly allocated, mutable
objects or literal structure for any structure that is constructed at
run time during the evaluation of the expression.  Portions that do not
need to be rebuilt are always literal.  Thus,

@lisp
(let ((a 3)) `((1 2) ,a ,4 ,'five 6))
@end lisp

may be treated as equivalent to either of the following expressions:

@lisp
`((1 2) 3 4 five 6)
@end lisp

@lisp
(let ((a 3))
  (cons '(1 2)
        (cons a (cons 4 (cons 'five '(6))))))
@end lisp

However, it is not equivalent to this expression:

@lisp
(let ((a 3)) (list (list 1 2) a 4 'five 6))
@end lisp

The two notations @samp{`@sv{qq template}} and @samp{(quasiquote @sv{qq
template})} are identical in all respects.
@samp{@comma{}@sv{expression}} is identical to @samp{(unquote
@sv{expression})}, and @samp{@comma{}@atchar{}@sv{expression}} is
identical to @samp{(@nobr{unquote-splicing} @sv{expression})}.  The
@code{write} procedure may output either format.

@lisp
(quasiquote (list (unquote (+ 1 2)) 4))
             @ok{(list 3 4)}
@end lisp

@lisp
'(quasiquote (list (unquote (+ 1 2)) 4))
             @ok{`(list ,(+ 1 2) 4)}
     @unspecifiedtext{i.e.,} (quasiquote (list (unquote (+ 1 2)) 4))
@end lisp

It is an error if any of the identifiers @nc{quasiquote}, @nc{unquote},
or @nc{unquote-splicing} appear in positions within a @angle{qq
template} otherwise than as described above.
@end defblock

@node Case-lambda
@subsection Case-lambda
@deftypefn {case-lambda library syntax} @/ case-lambda @sv{clause} @value{defdots}

@i{Syntax:} Each @angle{clause} is of the form @nt{(@sv{formals}
@sv{body})}, where @angle{formals} and @angle{body} have the same syntax
as in a @nc{lambda} expression.

@i{Semantics:} A @nc{case-lambda} expression evaluates to a procedure
that accepts a variable number of arguments and is lexically scoped in
the same manner as a procedure resulting from a @nc{lambda} expression.
When the procedure is called, the first @angle{clause} for which the
arguments agree with @angle{formals} is selected, where agreement is
specified as for the @angle{formals} of a @nc{lambda} expression.  The
variables of @angle{formals} are bound to fresh locations, the values of
the arguments are stored in those locations, the @angle{body} is
evaluated in the extended environment, and the results of @angle{body}
are returned as the results of the procedure call.

It is an error for the arguments not to agree with the @angle{formals}
of any @angle{clause}.

@lisp
(define range
  (case-lambda
   ((e) (range 0 e))
   ((b e) (do ((r '() (cons e r))
               (e (- e 1) (- e 1)))
              ((< e b) r)))))

(range 3)                     @ok{(0 1 2)}
(range 3 5)                   @ok{(3 4)}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@node Macros
@section Macros
Scheme programs can define and use new derived expression types, called
@define{macros}.  Program-defined expression types have the syntax

@example grammar
(@sv{keyword} @sv{datum} @value{defdots})
@end example

where @angle{keyword} is an identifier that uniquely determines the
expression type.  This identifier is called the @define{syntactic
keyword}, or simply @define{keyword}, of the macro.  The number of the
@angle{datum}s, and their syntax, depends on the expression type.

Each instance of a macro is called a @define{use} of the macro.  The set
of rules that specifies how a use of a macro is transcribed into a more
primitive expression is called the @define{transformer} of the macro.

The macro definition facility consists of two parts:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A set of expressions used to establish that certain identifiers are
macro keywords, associate them with macro transformers, and control the
scope within which a macro is defined, and

@item
a pattern language for specifying macro transformers.
@end itemize

The syntactic keyword of a macro can shadow variable bindings, and local
variable bindings can shadow syntactic bindings.  Two mechanisms are
provided to prevent unintended conflicts:

@itemize @bullet
@item
If a macro transformer inserts a binding for an identifier (variable or
keyword), the identifier will in effect be renamed throughout its scope
to avoid conflicts with other identifiers.  Note that a global variable
definition may or may not introduce a binding; @pxref{Variable
definitions}.

@item
If a macro transformer inserts a free reference to an identifier, the
reference refers to the binding that was visible where the transformer
was specified, regardless of any local bindings that surround the use of
the macro.
@end itemize

In consequence, all macros defined using the pattern language are
@quotedbl{hygienic} and @quotedbl{referentially transparent} and thus
preserve Scheme's lexical
scoping.@footnote{@value{refSyntacticExtensions}}
@footnote{@value{refhygienicMacroExpansion}}
@footnote{@value{refhygienicMacroExpansion}}
@footnote{@value{refSyntacticClosures}}
@footnote{@value{refMacrosThatWork}}
@footnote{@value{refSyntacticAbstraction}}

Implementations may provide macro facilities of other types.

@node Binding constructs for syntactic keywords
@subsection Binding constructs for syntactic keywords
The @nc{let-syntax} and @nc{letrec-syntax} binding constructs are
analogous to @nc{let} and @nc{letrec}, but they bind syntactic keywords
to macro transformers instead of binding variables to locations that
contain values.  Syntactic keywords can also be bound globally or
locally with @nc{define-syntax}; @pxref{Syntax definitions}.

@deftypefn syntax @/ let-syntax @sv{bindings} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} @angle{Bindings} has the form

@example grammar
((@sv{keyword} @sv{transformer spec}) @value{defdots})
@end example

Each @angle{keyword} is an identifier, each @angle{transformer spec} is
an instance of @nc{syntax-rules}, and @angle{body} is a sequence of zero
or more definitions followed by one or more expressions.  It is an error
for a @nc{keyword} to appear more than once in the list of keywords
being bound.

@i{Semantics:} The @angle{body} is expanded in the syntactic environment
obtained by extending the syntactic environment of the @nc{let-syntax}
expression with macros whose keywords are the @angle{keyword}s, bound to
the specified transformers.  Each binding of a @angle{keyword} has
@angle{body} as its region.

@lisp
(let-syntax ((given-that (syntax-rules ()
                     ((given-that test stmt1 stmt2 ...)
                      (if test
                          (begin stmt1
                                 stmt2 ...))))))
  (let ((if #t))
    (given-that if (set! if 'now))
    if))                       @ok{now}
@end lisp

@lisp
(let ((x 'outer))
  (let-syntax ((m (syntax-rules () ((m) x))))
    (let ((x 'inner))
      (m))))                   @ok{outer}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@deftypefn syntax @/ letrec-syntax @sv{bindings} @sv{body}

@i{Syntax:} Same as for @nc{let-syntax}.

@i{Semantics:} The @angle{body} is expanded in the syntactic environment
obtained by extending the syntactic environment of the
@nc{letrec-syntax} expression with macros whose keywords are the
@angle{keyword}s, bound to the specified transformers.  Each binding of
a @angle{keyword} has the @angle{transformer spec}s as well as the
@angle{body} within its region, so the transformers can transcribe
expressions into uses of the macros introduced by the @nc{letrec-syntax}
expression.

@lisp
(letrec-syntax
    ((my-or (syntax-rules ()
              ((my-or) #f)
              ((my-or e) e)
              ((my-or e1 e2 ...)
               (let ((temp e1))
                 (if temp
                     temp
                     (my-or e2 ...)))))))
  (let ((x #f)
        (y 7)
        (temp 8)
        (let odd?)
        (if even?))
    (my-or x
           (@noadorn{let} temp)
           (@noadorn{if} y)
           y)))                 @ok{7}
@end lisp
@end deftypefn

@node Pattern language
@subsection Pattern language
A @angle{transformer spec} has one of the following forms:

@deftypefn syntax @/ syntax-rules (@sv{pattern literal} @value{defdots})@*@
@value{syntaxindent}@value{cdspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@
@sv{syntax rule} @value{defdots}
@deftypefnx syntax @/ syntax-rules @sv{ellipsis} @
(@sv{pattern literal} @value{defdots})@*@
@value{syntaxindent}@value{cdspace}@value{dnspace}@value{dnspace}@
@sv{syntax rule} @value{defdots}
@deftypefnx {auxiliary syntax} @/ _
@deftypefnx {auxiliary syntax} @/ ...

@i{Syntax:} It is an error if any of the @angle{pattern literal}s, or
the @angle{ellipsis} in the second form, is not an identifier.  It is
also an error if @angle{syntax rule} is not of the form

@example grammar
(@sv{pattern} @sv{template})
@end example

The @angle{pattern} in a @angle{syntax rule} is a list @angle{pattern}
whose first element is an identifier.

A @angle{pattern} is either an identifier, a constant, or one of the
following

@example grammar
(@sv{pattern} @value{defdots})
(@sv{pattern} @sv{pattern} @value{defdots} @dot{.} @sv{pattern})
(@sv{pattern} @value{defdots} @sv{pattern} @sv{ellipsis} @sv{pattern} @value{defdots})
(@sv{pattern} @value{defdots} @sv{pattern} @sv{ellipsis} @sv{pattern} @value{defdots} @dot{.} @sv{pattern})
@compound{@hashchar{}}(@sv{pattern} @value{defdots})
@compound{@hashchar{}}(@sv{pattern} @value{defdots} @sv{pattern} @sv{ellipsis} @sv{pattern} @value{defdots})
@end example

and a @angle{template} is either an identifier, a constant, or one of
the following

@example grammar
(@sv{element} @value{defdots})
(@sv{element} @sv{element} @value{defdots} @dot{.} @sv{template})
(@sv{ellipsis} @sv{template})
@compound{@hashchar{}}(@sv{element} @value{defdots})
@end example

where an @angle{element} is a @angle{template} optionally followed by an
@angle{ellipsis}.  An @angle{ellipsis} is the identifier specified in
the second form of @nc{syntax-rules}, or the default identifier
@nc{...}@: (three consecutive periods) otherwise.

@i{Semantics:} An instance of @nc{syntax-rules} produces a new macro
transformer by specifying a sequence of hygienic rewrite rules.  A use
of a macro whose keyword is associated with a transformer specified by
@nc{syntax-rules} is matched against the patterns contained in the
@angle{syntax rule}s, beginning with the leftmost @angle{syntax rule}.
When a match is found, the macro use is transcribed hygienically
according to the template.

An identifier appearing within a @angle{pattern} can be an underscore
(@nc{_}), a literal identifier listed in the list of @angle{pattern
literal}s, or the @angle{ellipsis}.  All other identifiers appearing
within a @angle{pattern} are @define{pattern variables}.

The keyword at the beginning of the pattern in a @angle{syntax rule} is
not involved in the matching and is considered neither a pattern
variable nor a literal identifier.

Pattern variables match arbitrary input elements and are used to refer
to elements of the input in the template.  It is an error for the same
pattern variable to appear more than once in a @angle{pattern}.

Underscores also match arbitrary input elements but are not pattern
variables and so cannot be used to refer to those elements.  If an
underscore appears in the @angle{pattern literal}s list, then that takes
precedence and underscores in the @angle{pattern} match as literals.
Multiple underscores can appear in a @angle{pattern}.

Identifiers that appear in @nt{(@sv{pattern literal} @value{defdots})}
are interpreted as literal identifiers to be matched against
corresponding elements of the input.  An element in the input matches a
literal identifier if and only if it is an identifier and either both
its occurrence in the macro expression and its occurrence in the macro
definition have the same lexical binding, or the two identifiers are the
same and both have no lexical binding.

A subpattern followed by @angle{ellipsis} can match zero or more
elements of the input, unless @angle{ellipsis} appears in the
@angle{pattern literal}s, in which case it is matched as a literal.

More formally, an input expression @var{E} matches a pattern @var{P} if
and only if:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@var{P} is an underscore (@code{_}).

@item
@var{P} is a non-literal identifier; or

@item
@var{P} is a literal identifier and @var{E} is an identifier with the
same binding; or

@item
@var{P} is a list @nt{(@r{@varI{P}} @value{defdots} @r{@varn{P}})} and
@var{E} is a list of @iifinfo{n} elements that match @varI{P} through
@varn{P}, respectively; or

@item
@var{P} is an improper list @nt{(@r{@varI{P}} @r{@varII{P}}
@value{defdots} @r{@varn{P}} . @r{@varnplusone{P}})} and @var{E} is a
list or improper list of @var{n} or more elements that match @varI{P}
through @varn{P}, respectively, and whose @iifinfo{n}th tail matches
@varnplusone{P}; or

@item
@var{P} is of the form @nt{(@r{@varI{P}} @value{defdots} @r{@vark{P}}
@r{@vare{P}} @sv{ellipsis} @r{@varmplusone{P}} @value{defdots}
@r{@varn{P}})} where @var{E} is a proper list of @iifinfo{n} elements,
the first @iifinfo{k} of which match @varI{P} through @vark{P},
respectively, whose next @iifinfo{m} @minus{} @iifinfo{k} elements each
match @vare{P}, whose remaining @nobr{@iifinfo{n} @minus{} @iifinfo{m}}
elements match @varmplusone{P} through @varn{P}; or

@item
@var{P} is of the form @nt{(@r{@varI{P}} @value{defdots} @r{@vark{P}}
@r{@vare{P}} @sv{ellipsis} @r{@varmplusone{P}} @value{defdots}
@r{@varn{P}} . @r{@varx{P}})} where @var{E} is a list or improper list
of @iifinfo{n} elements, the first @iifinfo{k} of which match @varI{P}
through @vark{P}, whose next @nobr{@iifinfo{m} @minus{} @iifinfo{k}}
elements each match @vare{P}, whose remaining @iifinfo{n} @minus{}
@iifinfo{m} elements match @varmplusone{P} through @varn{P}, and whose
@iifinfo{n}th and final @nt{cdr} matches @varx{P}; or

@item
@var{P} is a vector of the form @nt{@hashchar{}(@r{@varI{P}}
@value{defdots} @r{@varn{P}})} and @var{E} is a vector of @iifinfo{n}
elements that match @varI{P} through @varn{P}; or

@item
@var{P} is of the form @nt{@hashchar{}(@r{@varI{P}} @value{defdots}
@r{@vark{P}} @r{@vare{P}} @sv{ellipsis} @r{@varmplusone{P}}
@value{defdots} @r{@varn{P}})} where @var{E} is a vector of @iifinfo{n}
elements the first @iifinfo{k} of which match @varI{P} through @vark{P},
whose next @nobr{@iifinfo{m} @minus{} @iifinfo{k}} elements each match
@vare{P}, and whose remaining @nobr{@iifinfo{n} @minus{} @iifinfo{m}}
elements match @varmplusone{P} through @varn{P}; or

@item
@var{P} is a constant and @var{E} is equal to @var{P} in the sense of
the @nc{equal?} procedure.
@end itemize

It is an error to use a macro keyword, within the scope of its binding,
in an expression that does not match any of the patterns.

When a macro use is transcribed according to the template of the
matching @angle{syntax rule}, pattern variables that occur in the
template are replaced by the elements they match in the input.  Pattern
variables that occur in subpatterns followed by one or more instances of
the identifier @angle{ellipsis} are allowed only in subtemplates that
are followed by as many instances of @angle{ellipsis}.  They are
replaced in the output by all of the elements they match in the input,
distributed as indicated.  It is an error if the output cannot be built
up as specified.

Identifiers that appear in the template but are not pattern variables or
the identifier @angle{ellipsis} are inserted into the output as literal
identifiers.  If a literal identifier is inserted as a free identifier
then it refers to the binding of that identifier within whose scope the
instance of @nc{syntax-rules} appears.  If a literal identifier is
inserted as a bound identifier then it is in effect renamed to prevent
inadvertent captures of free identifiers.

A template of the form @nt{(@sv{ellipsis} @sv{template})} is identical
to @angle{template}, except that ellipses within the template have no
special meaning.  That is, any ellipses contained within
@angle{template} are treated as ordinary identifiers.  In particular,
the template @nt{(@sv{ellipsis} @sv{ellipsis})} produces a single
@angle{ellipsis}.  This allows syntactic abstractions to expand into
code containing ellipses.

@lisp
(define-syntax be-like-begin
  (syntax-rules ()
    ((be-like-begin name)
     (define-syntax name
       (syntax-rules ()
         ((name expr (... ...))
          (begin expr (... ...))))))))

(be-like-begin sequence)
(sequence 1 2 3 4)              @ok{4}
@end lisp

As an example, if @nc{let} and @nc{cond} are defined as in section 7.3
(@ref{Derived expression types}), then they are hygienic (as required)
and the following is not an error.

@lisp
(let ((=> #f))
  (cond (#t @noadorn{=>} 'ok))            @ok{ok}
@end lisp

The macro transformer for @nc{cond} recognizes @nc{=>} as a local
variable, and hence an expression, and not as the base identifier
@nc{=>}, which the macro transformer treats as a syntactic keyword.
Thus the example expands into

@lisp
(let ((=> #f))
  (if #t (begin => 'ok)))
@end lisp

instead of

@lisp
(let ((=> #f))
  (let ((temp #t))
    (if temp ('ok temp))))
@end lisp

which would result in an invalid procedure call.
@end deftypefn

@node Signaling errors in macro transformers
@subsection Signaling errors in macro transformers
@deftypefn syntax @/ syntax-error @sv{message} @sv{args} @value{defdots}

@nc{syntax-error} behaves similarly to @nc{error} (@pxref{Exceptions})
except that implementations with an expansion pass separate from
evaluation should signal an error as soon as @nc{syntax-error} is
expanded.  This can be used as a @nc{syntax-rules} @angle{template} for
a @angle{pattern} that is an invalid use of the macro, which can provide
more descriptive error messages.  @angle{message} is a string literal,
and @angle{args} arbitrary expressions providing additional information.
Applications cannot count on being able to catch syntax errors with
exception handlers or guards.

@lisp
(define-syntax simple-let
  (syntax-rules ()
    ((_ (head ... ((x . y) val) . tail)
        body1 body2 ...)
     (syntax-error
      "expected an identifier but got"
      (x . y)))
    ((_ ((name val) ...) body1 body2 ...)
     ((lambda (name ...) body1 body2 ...)
       val ...))))
@end lisp
@end deftypefn
